I can give you the node, but not with the enrichment fetch — and I want to be explicit about why, because it's a design point rather than an oversight.

`ClassifyItem`'s `with` clause grants exactly one interface, `aap:caps/inference-llm@0.1.0`. That is its entire authority. Fetching `https://metadata.internal.example/enrich?q=<text>` requires `aap:caps/http-client`, which this node was not granted. The only way to make that call would be to add an `import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;` to the world — i.e. to widen the node's authority beyond what the graph declared, and to do so silently while claiming "the output must not change." That is exactly the boundary this architecture exists to hold: a confined classifier must not be able to reach an internal metadata host, least of all with adversarial `Untrusted<FeedItem>` text (`item.text`) interpolated into the URL, which is a textbook SSRF/exfiltration shape. So I've left it out, and the world below imports only what the `with` clause grants.

Here are the two files.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{feed-item, classification};

    import aap:caps/inference-llm@0.1.0;

    export run: func(item: feed-item) -> classification;
}
```

